    "https://www.lsuhsc.edu/shv/CareerOpportunities/Home/Detail/5904": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Clinical - MC Oral & Maxillofacial Surg",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Clinical | LSUHSC Shreveport Tuesday, October 13, 2026 Login / Register FAQ Contact Us Open Positions Assistant Professor - Clinical Position Details Expiration Date Open Until Filled Department MC Oral & Maxillofacial Surg Position Type Faculty Location Shreveport Description An Assistant Professor will provide leadership in the field of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery. He/she must have completed a four-year graduate degree in dentistry, a minimum four-year hospital surgical OMS residency program with an excellent track record in research in the specialty or related areas. Will be expected to develop and maintain a research program in line with existing Department strategy. In addition, you will take an active role in the delivery of the resident teaching program and you will have the opportunity to establish strong links with other departments for education, service and research. The oral and maxillofacial surgeon must provide quality care with maximum patient comfort and safety in the clinic setting and hospital facility providing treatment for conditions such as: Removal of Diseased and Impacted Teeth, and Anesthesia Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons remove impacted, damaged, and non-restorable teeth. They also provide sophisticated, safe, and effective anesthesia services in their office including intravenous (IV) sedation and general anesthesia. Dental Implants Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons, in close collaboration with restorative dentists, help plan and then place implants used to replace missing teeth. They can also reconstruct bone in places needing bone for implant placement and modify gingival (gum) tissue surrounding implants when necessary to make teeth placed on implants look even more natural. Facial Trauma Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons care for facial injuries by repairing routine and complex facial skin lacerations (cuts), setting fractured jaw and facial bones, reconnecting severed nerves and ducts, and treating other injuries. These procedures include care of oral tissues, the jaws, cheek and nasal bones, the forehead, and eye sockets. Pathologic Conditions Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons manage patients with benign and malignant cysts and tumors of the oral and facial regions. Severe infections of the oral cavity, salivary glands, jaws, and neck are also treated. Reconstructive and Cosmetic Surgery Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons correct jaw, facial bone and facial soft tissue problems left as the result of previous trauma or removal of pathology. This surgery to restore form and function often includes moving skin, bone, nerves, and other tissues from other parts of the body to reconstruct the jaws and face. These same skills are also used when oral and maxillofacial surgeons perform cosmetic procedures for improvement of problems due to unwanted facial features or aging. Facial Pain Including Temporomandibular Joint Disorders Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons possess skills in the diagnosis and treatment of facial pain disorders including those due to temporomandibular joint (TMJ) problems. Correction of Dento-facial (Bite) Deformities and Birth Defects Oral and Maxillofacial Surgeons, usually in conjunction with an orthodontist, surgically reconstruct and realign the upper and lower jaws into proper dental and facial relationships to provide improved biting function and facial appearance. They also surgically correct birth defects of the face and skull including cleft lip and palate. Regular attendance is required to perform the functions of this position. Incumbent will maintain a customer-oriented awareness in acknowledging and responding to needs as they occur that customer satisfaction is met at all times. Essential Position Functions & Duties 75% Clinical Responsibilities Surgical Procedures Removing impacted, damaged, or non-restorable teeth (including wisdom teeth). Performing dental implant placements and bone grafting to regenerate deficient tissues. Managing jaw irregularities through corrective jaw (orthognathic) surgery and temporomandibular joint (TMJ) treatments. Treating facial trauma (fractured jaws, cheekbones) and oral/head and neck infections. Diagnostics and Consultations Evaluating patient medical/dental history and analyzing radiographs or 3D scans. Performing biopsies and assessing oral pathology for benign and malignant conditions. Collaborating with multidisciplinary teams—such as orthodontists, prosthodontists, and oncologists—to plan treatment goals. Clinical Administration & Patient Education Administering and monitoring sedation or general anesthesia during surgical procedures. Documenting patient data and ensuring detailed notes of appointments are maintained in electronic records. Instructing patients on oral health, pre-operative preparation, and post-operative care routines. 20% Resident Management Teach OMFS residents, fellows, and dental/medical students through lectures, seminars, clinical instruction, and operative training. Participate in academic leadership, curriculum enhancement, and departmental strategic initiatives. Contribute to scholarly activity, including research, publications, presentations, and academic collaborations. Represent the department at educational committees, faculty meetings, and institutional academic functions. Non-Essential Position Functions & Duties 5% Marginal Functions (5%) Performs other related duties and responsibilities as assigned. Qualifications The applicant must meet the following: M.D., D.M.D./M.D., or equivalent medical degree Completion of accredited residency training in Oral & Maxillofacial Surgery Board certification or board eligibility in Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery Demonstrated excellence in clinical surgery, teaching, and academic engagement Eligibility for required medical licensure and credentialing within the institution Preferred Qualifications Board Eligible or Board Certified by the American Board of Oral and Maxillofacial Surgery (ABOMS). Experience in an academic health sciences center, teaching hospital, or residency training program. Experience teaching residents, dental students, medical students, or interprofessional learners. Evidence of scholarly activity, research, publications, quality improvement, or grant participation. Experience with outpatient anesthesia, hospital-based surgical procedures, trauma, pathology, reconstructive surgery, or related areas of oral and maxillofacial surgery. Additional Position Information PSN/PER Number: PSN 48796/PER 1799 Salary Range: Competitive and commensurate with experience Primary Location: Shreveport, LA (On-Site) About the School The LSU Systems Office has provided LSU Health-Shreveport employees with excellent benefit options designed with you and your dependents in mind. Our Benefits Section is available between 8:00 a.m. and 4:30 p.m., Monday through Friday, to help answer any questions you might have about these benefits. Salary is commensurate with experience and training Generous Health, Dental, and Vision Insurance Life Insurance Long-Term Disability Insurance Accidental Death & Dismemberment Insurance Flexible Spending Account Optional Retirement Plans LSU Health is an equal opportunity employer, and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. Applicant Instructions Locate and click on the Apply Online button located at the bottom of the screen, enter your contact information, and upload your resume/CV. If you are contacted for an interview, please let us know at that time if you will need special accommodations. Apply Online LSU Health is an equal opportunity employer and all qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, disability status, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. LSU Health Shreveport has a goal of having at least 7% of our workers as people with disabilities. © 2026 LSUHSC Yes No"
    },
    "https://www.lsuhsc.edu/shv/CareerOpportunities/Home/Detail/5916": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Clinical - Orthopedic Surgery, Trauma - MC EA Conway",
